{
    "html": "# mikanBox Page Production Rules (Sample)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> This file is a sample. Rename its ID to `AGENTS.md` and AI agents will read it as\n> your production rules. As long as it stays `AGENTS_sample.md` it affects nothing.\n> Rewrite the contents to match how your site is run. Design rules such as colors\n> and dimensions belong in `DESIGN.md`.\n\n## 1. Page and component IDs\n\n- A page ID becomes its URL. Use letters, digits, `-` and `_`, and keep it short\n  and meaningful.\n- **Including `\/` in an ID creates a hierarchy.** For example `news\/2026-spring`\n  becomes `SITE_URL\/news\/2026-spring`, and a static build creates a real\n  `news\/2026-spring\/` folder. When you need the parent page at `\/news\/`, create it\n  with the ID `news`, without a slash (not `news\/` and not `news\/index`).\n- Components whose ID starts with `_` are the standard parts bundled with mikanBox.\n  **You are free to rewrite their contents** — they are meant to grow with your site.\n  But **do not change their IDs**: references like `{{COMPONENT:_header}}` and the\n  bundled pages will break.\n- Do not prefix new components with `_`. Give them a name that says what they do,\n  such as `card_news` or `cta_contact`.\n\n## 2. Using the separate fields\n\nPages and components have separate \"Content\", \"CSS\" and \"JavaScript\" fields.\n**Except where the structure is unusually complex, keep them in their own fields.**\n\n- CSS goes in the CSS field. Do not write `<style>` in the content.\n- JavaScript goes in the JavaScript field. Do not write `<script>` in the content.\n- Why: CSS in the CSS field is scoped automatically, so it cannot affect other pages\n  or parts, and the site's CSS is collected and output at the `{{HEAD_CSS}}` position.\n  The JavaScript field is output once per page for each component ID. Writing either\n  one directly in the content loses both behaviors.\n- mikanBox is a system for managing content. There is no need to force a tightly\n  coupled, web-app-like structure into separate fields. In that case, set the Design\n  Component to \"None (use the content HTML directly)\" and put the whole HTML document\n  in the content.\n- \"AI instruction\" components (such as this file) have no JavaScript field, and are\n  never served or executed as JavaScript.\n\n## 3. Write the content in Markdown wherever you can\n\nNot for appearance, but because it keeps the content **easy to read, easy to reuse\nand easy to correct**. Once it is buried in HTML, editing just the text, or reusing\nit on another page, becomes difficult.\n\n- Use Markdown for headings, paragraphs, lists, links, emphasis, tables and code blocks.\n- Use HTML only for wrappers Markdown cannot express (grids, cards, sections). Even\n  then, the text inside can still be written in Markdown.\n- Do not stack empty `<div>` elements for decoration. Add one class name and build the\n  appearance in the CSS field.\n\n## 4. Turn shared parts into components\n\n- Headers, footers, shared tags inside `<head>`, navigation and repeating cards should\n  not be written into pages directly. Make them components and call them with\n  `{{COMPONENT:ID}}`.\n- Standard parts: `_layout` (the wrapper for the whole page), `_global_head`, `_header`,\n  `_footer`, `_nav_card`. Regular pages select `_layout` as the Design Component.\n- As soon as the same appearance shows up on two or more pages, split it into a component.\n- Do not hand-write page lists; use `{{NAV_CARDS}}` \/ `{{NAV_LINKS}}`.\n\n### Building an index page\n\nCategory index pages are not created automatically.\n\n- Articles: give them IDs like `news\/2026-spring` and put `news` in their categories.\n  A page whose \"Display order\" is below 0 does not appear in lists.\n- The index: create a page with the ID `news` and place\n  `{{NAV_CARDS:news:_nav_card}}` in its content (or `{{NAV_LINKS:news}}` for a list of\n  links). That gives you `\/news\/` as the index and `\/news\/2026-spring\/` as the article.\n  Neither `news\/` nor `news\/list` is needed.\n- Lists include the current page, because navigation needs to show where you are. So do\n  not give the index page itself the `news` category. If you also want it in the header\n  navigation, give it a separate category such as `nav`.\n- When you place a list of the same category at the bottom of an article as\n  \"other articles\", the current page is always included. Adding\n  `.nav-card.active { display: none; }` (or `li.active { display: none; }` for links)\n  to the card component's CSS field hides it.\n- For pages published as HTML, rebuild the index page whenever you add or remove an article.\n\n## 5. Images and external files come from Media\n\n- Upload images, audio and video from the Media screen (or with MCP `upload_media`).\n  Do not assemble paths yourself.\n  - Markdown: `![description](filename)`, `{{IMAGE:filename}}`, `{{AUDIO:filename}}`,\n    `{{VIDEO:filename}}`\n  - HTML: `media\/filename` (for example `<img src=\"media\/photo.jpg\">`)\n- **External JS and CSS files can be uploaded to Media too** (UTF-8 text, non-empty,\n  up to 10 MB per file). You write the references yourself:\n  - `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"media\/library-1.2.3.min.css\">`\n  - `<script src=\"media\/library-1.2.3.min.js\" defer><\/script>`\n- Uploading alone does not insert anything into a page. `media\/` paths are completed to\n  match the page's depth and are included in a static export.\n- Common libraries such as jQuery can be loaded from a CDN. Putting a copy in Media is\n  the option for pinning a version, removing an external dependency, or keeping a static\n  export entirely self-contained.\n\n## 6. Publication status and builds\n\n**Static generation and dynamic rendering are chosen per page, and can be mixed within\none site.**\n\n- **Public (dynamic)** — generated on each request. Updates appear as soon as you save.\n- **Public (HTML)** — published as a static HTML file. Frequently updated pages can stay\n  dynamic while stable, speed-sensitive pages are static.\n- **Draft** \/ **Pending** \/ **DB** — not public. `DB` exposes the page's `{{DATAROW}}`\n  data as an API at `SITE_URL\/api\/PAGE_ID`.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **Editing and saving a Public (HTML) page does not update the published site.**\n> Run the static site generation (SSG) build. Over MCP, call `build_ssg`. When you change\n> a shared part or its CSS, the static pages that use it need rebuilding too. Changing a\n> Public (HTML) page to any other status deletes its exported static HTML.\n\n## 7. Final checks\n\n- [ ] No CSS or JavaScript written into the content\n- [ ] The content's HTML is kept to a minimum and the text is Markdown\n- [ ] New component IDs do not start with `_`, and no standard part's ID was changed\n- [ ] Shared parts are components\n- [ ] Images, JS and CSS go through Media, and HTML references them as `media\/filename`\n- [ ] A Design Component is selected (\"None\" only for a single-file HTML page)\n- [ ] Public (HTML) pages were rebuilt after editing\n- [ ] Colors and dimensions follow `DESIGN.md`",
